cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use these replication materials, please cite the paper."
title: >-
  Replication materials — The Information Costs of Climate Misinformation:
  Evidence from Global Financial Data
abstract: >-
  Replication code and derived data for a study of whether firm-day exposure
  to sector-specific climate misinformation moves the trading costs of 204
  listed climate-transition firms (2012-2025). Exposure is built from
  1,959,707 judgments by Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-FP8, which scored 217,954 Facebook
  climate posts for relevance to each of nine TRBC energy sub-industries and
  for climate-or-energy misinformation.
authors:
  - family-names: Rice
    given-names: James K.
    affiliation: University of Essex
version: "2.0.0"
date-released: "2026-09-30"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - climate misinformation
  - information asymmetry
  - market microstructure
  - bid-ask spread
  - large language models
  - replication package
